1. Create a free AWS account

Go to the Amazon Web Services (AWS) website and sign up if you don’t already have an account. 
Amazon Web Services, Inc.
+1

Once signed in, open the AWS Management Console.

Ensure you have access to the S3 service.

2. Open S3 service

In the console search bar, type “S3” and select the S3 service. 
Amazon Web Services, Inc.
+1

Ensure you’re in the region you want (you can pick e.g., us‐east‑1 or your preferred region). 
AWS Documentation

3. Create a bucket

Click “Create bucket” in the S3 console. 
AWS Documentation
+1

Enter a bucket name — it must be globally unique across all AWS S3 buckets. 
AWS Documentation
+1

Choose your Region (e.g., us‑east‑1).

Leave other settings as the defaults (you don’t need to change permissions or advanced config for this simple task). 
Amazon Web Services, Inc.
+1

Click “Create bucket”. Your bucket is now ready. 
AWS Documentation
+1

4. Upload a sample file

In your bucket list, click the name of the bucket you just created to go into it. 
AWS Documentation

Click “Upload”. 
AWS Documentation
+1

Select “Add files” (or drag & drop) to choose a file from your computer (for example a .txt or image file). 
AWS Documentation

Leave the default permission and property settings (for this task) unless you want to make the file publicly accessible. 
Amazon Web Services, Inc.

Click “Upload” (or “Start upload”). Wait until you see that the object is listed in the bucket. 
AWS Documentation
+1

5. (Optional) Make the file public and test URL

If you want the file to be accessible via a browser link, you’ll need to allow public access (or at least the object should have public read).

After upload, click the uploaded object, and you’ll see the object URL (a web link like https://bucket‑name.s3.region.amazonaws.com/yourfile) — copy that link. 
Amazon Web Services, Inc.
+1

Paste the URL into a browser to ensure the file loads/open successfully. This verifies accessibility.

6. Capture a screenshot (for your deliverable)

While you’re viewing your bucket and seeing the uploaded file listed, take a screenshot of that screen (showing bucket name + file).

Make sure the screenshot clearly shows the bucket list with the file name and possibly the object URL or status.

Save the screenshot for submission.

7. Summary / Outcome

You have created a bucket (container) in AWS S3.

You have uploaded a file (object) into that bucket.

You optionally made the file publicly accessible and tested its URL in a browser.

You now understand the fundamentals of “object storage” (bucket = storage container, objects = files inside it) in a cloud context. 
